Id: sudo004w
Severity: WARN
Category: accounts
Check: check_sudo
Fix: List the exact commands and arguments instead of a wildcard.
References: sudoers(5)
Controls: CIS 5.4; NIST AC-6; ISO 8.2; CE User access control

A sudo rule names a command with a wildcard (*). In sudoers a *
matches any characters, spaces included, so a rule meant for one kind
of call allows others: "/bin/cat /var/log/*" also runs
"/bin/cat /var/log/../../etc/shadow", and "/usr/bin/systemctl
restart *" accepts any further options. The sudoers manual warns
against wildcards in arguments for this reason.

To fix it, list the exact commands and arguments allowed, one per
entry, or give the user a script of root's that does the one thing and
accepts no arguments.
